sfwbar for FlickOS
==================

Upstream: https://github.com/LBCrion/sfwbar (GPL-3). Debian trixie doesn't
have it (Debian's package stopped at 1.0~beta13 and was removed), so FlickOS
packages it natively. It is the only compiled FlickOS package
(Architecture: any, built with meson by debian/rules).

This folder is upstream's source tree at git commit d40912d (main,
2026-09-10), without .github/, packaging/, .gitattributes and
doc/sfwbar.rst.bak.

Changes to upstream (marked "FlickOS:" in the code). Each one is also kept
as a patch against d40912d in debian/flickos-patches/, in the order of this
list (series names them). They are a record for updating, not applied at
build (a native package's source is already patched): applied in order to
upstream d40912d, they give exactly this tree. A new change adds a patch
there (its diff, made with git format-patch or diff -u from the tree root,
paths a/src/...) and a line to series, as well as an entry here.

  - src/gui/taskbarpopup.c: taskbar_popup_update() sets the CSS class
    "running" on an app's button while it has a window, not only its pin.
    flickos-layouts' Cupertino dock.css draws the open-app line with it.
  - src/gui/bar.c, bar.h: bar property sensor_maximized (boolean). With a
    sensor, the bar stays shown and hides only while the focused window is
    maximized or fullscreen on the bar's output (bar_may_hide). A window
    listener re-checks all bars after window changes. Not in the man pages
    (doc/ isn't rebuilt).
  - src/ipc/foreign-toplevel.c: toplevel_handle_state() calls
    wintree_commit(), so listeners hear about state changes such as
    maximizing (upstream only notified them on focus changes).
  - src/workspace.c: workspace_mod_state() compares only the flags it
    changes. Upstream compared all state flags, so once one was set (every
    shown workspace is "visible") no other could be, and the pager never
    marked the active workspace "focused" (seen with labwc's ext-workspace).
    flickos-layouts' Traditional desktop switcher highlights it.
  - src/gui/bar.c: no longer forces gtk-enable-animations on, so a bar
    follows GSettings' enable-animations (FlickOS's Settings >
    Accessibility > Animations). bar_sensor_hide() finishes the hide at
    once when the revealer hid without an animation: upstream connected
    its notify::child-revealed handler after the revealer had already
    notified, so the sensor never came back (the reason, it seems, for
    forcing animations on; transition = 0 hit it too).
  - src/lib/compositor.c: WindowInfo() takes a taskbar group button's
    window (group = popup) from the action, as Focus() and Minimize() do.
    Upstream read the group's app id as a window_t, so
    WindowInfo('focused') was false and a click on the Cupertino dock never
    minimized the focused app (TaskbarItemDefault).
  - src/sfwbar.c: activate() starts the scanner thread after expr_init()
    and scanner_init(), as upstream does since 88c83b9. Started before
    them, the thread's first scanner_invalidate() could lock the still
    NULL scan_list: a segfault at address 8 in thread "scanner" now and
    then at start, seen when a layout switch started the dock. Drop this
    entry when updating past 88c83b9.
  - src/gui/bar.c: bar_resize() asks for no length for a full-length bar
    (anchored to both ends), so it takes the length the compositor gives
    it. Upstream asked for the monitor's length, so the Footprint layout's
    left dock, below the top bar's exclusive zone, ran 30 px past the
    bottom of the screen and cut off its Show Apps button.
  - src/appinfo.c, src/gui/scaleimage.c: app_info_icon_get() returns a
    desktop file's Icon= when it is a readable file's full path, and
    scale_image_set() draws such a result as a file (SI_FILE). Upstream
    looked every Icon= up in the icon theme, which only knows names, so
    Firefox's web apps (Icon= a PNG in the profile) got the default icon
    in the Cupertino dock and Traditional's window list.
  - src/gui/taskbarpopup.c, src/lib/compositor.c: with group = popup, a
    right click (slot 3) always runs the taskbar's RightClick action
    (upstream ignored it on a pin without a window and on a group of several
    windows), and WindowInfo() on such a button answers "appid" (the
    group's app) and "pinned" (1 while the app is pinned). The Footprint
    dock's Pin to Dock / Unpin from Dock menu is built on them:
    action[RightClick] = Menu(If(WindowInfo("pinned"), ...)), and a menu
    item's WindowInfo("appid") reads the clicked button, as a menu runs with
    its caller's widget.
  - src/gui/flowgrid.c, flowgrid.h, taskbarshell.c/.h, tray.c/.h,
    pager.c/.h: no 100 ms poll. Upstream's taskbars, trays and pagers each
    ran flow_grid_update() every 100 ms, which did nothing unless the grid
    was invalid (~600 wakeups a minute per grid on an idle desktop). They
    call flow_grid_set_auto_update() instead, and flow_grid_invalidate()
    starts a one-shot 100 ms timeout on such a grid, so changes are still
    batched and shown within 100 ms.
  - src/vm/vm.c, vm.h, src/gui/basewidget.c: vm_code_is_constant(), and
    base_widget_update_list() doesn't poll a widget whose value and style
    are constant (only immediate values and operators). Upstream
    re-evaluated them every second (the default interval), including the
    "layout" style of every bar's grid, so every bar woke its scanner
    thread once a second. Widgets with variables, functions or If() still
    poll as before.
  - src/gui/basewidget.c: base_widget_style() names each mirror's child,
    as it names its own. Upstream named the mirror widget itself, which CSS
    doesn't match, so a bar's copies on other screens (mirror = "*") kept
    the style a widget had when the copy was made: a tiler button never
    dimmed there, and a bar ":end" section declared before the bar lost its
    styles on the copies.
  - src/appinfo.c: app_info_lookup_id() tries the app's own desktop file
    (exactly APP_ID.desktop) before an icon named like the app id, as
    waybar does; the fuzzy desktop file search stays after the icon name.
    Upstream tried the icon name first, so Thunar (app id "thunar",
    Icon=org.xfce.thunar) got Papirus' old "thunar" icon in the Cupertino
    dock and Traditional's window list.
  - src/gui/basewidget.c: base_widget_scanner_thread() skips a widget
    whose interval is 0. base_widget_set_trigger() sets interval 0 before
    base_widget_update_list() takes widget_mutex to remove the widget; in
    between, upstream polled it with next_poll always "now", so the thread
    never slept, kept retaking the mutex and starved the main thread: the
    config parse hung at 100% CPU and no bar was drawn (a polled widget
    given a trigger, such as Redmond sfwbar's weather grid).
  - src/gui/basewidget.c: base_widget_style_apply() reads a style
    "name.class1.class2" as the widget's name plus CSS classes, and takes
    off the classes the style set last time. Upstream's "class" property is
    one fixed string, so a state (paused, stale, recording) needed a style
    name per state; with this the config can say
    style = "media_title." + $Class, the way waybar's JSON class works,
    and add-ons' CSS (#custom-trail.paused) applies as written. When the
    classes change it emits style-updated on the widget's children, since
    GTK emits it only where CSS values changed, and a ScaleImage colored
    by a style property alone (-ScaleImage-color) kept its old color.
  - src/gui/basewidget.c: a base widget sets the prelight state on its
    child while the pointer is inside it (enter/leave events, crossings
    to a widget inside ignored, cleared on unmap), unless the child is a
    button, which does that itself. GTK 3 sets prelight only where a
    widget does so itself, so CSS :hover on a grid, label or image
    (grid#weather:hover in Redmond sfwbar) never matched upstream.
  - src/gui/flowgrid.c, flowgrid.h, popup.c, popup.h: flow grid property
    overflow (boolean, one-row grids only, bound on mirrors). The items
    that fit the width the bar gives stay in it; the rest go to a pop-up
    list (window#taskbar_more, grid#taskbar_more_grid: one row, wrapped at
    the screen's width) that a more button after the last item
    (button#taskbar_more, the view-more-horizontal-symbolic icon, in an
    event box so the list opens at it) opens and closes on a click. The
    list closes on Escape, a click outside it (it grabs, popup_popdown()
    is no longer static), and a left or middle click on one of its items.
    The grid asks for no minimum width and for all its items' natural
    width, and size_allocate works out how many fit in the width the bar
    gives it (not the inner grid's, which halign start keeps as narrow as
    its items). Upstream's grid grew
    with every item and pushed the rest of the bar off the screen.
  - src/gui/tray.c: tray_init() no longer adds the existing tray items
    after sni_listener_register(), which adds them already. Upstream added
    them twice, so a tray made after its items (a mirrored bar's copy on a
    second screen) showed each one twice, such as two nm-applet icons on
    Redmond+'s panel on a laptop's own screen.

Why a git commit and not a release: the latest release, 1.0_beta17, lacks
taskbar pins (documented but only implemented for the pager) and its
size = "auto" bars collapse to 1px. Both work in d40912d. Switch to the
first release after beta17 once it is out.

Updating to upstream commit or release REF:

  1. git clone https://github.com/LBCrion/sfwbar && cd sfwbar
  2. Replace everything here except debian/ with
     git archive REF | tar -x --exclude=.github --exclude=packaging \
         --exclude=.gitattributes --exclude=doc/sfwbar.rst.bak
  3. Apply debian/flickos-patches/ in series order
     (while read p; do patch -p1 < debian/flickos-patches/$p; done
     < debian/flickos-patches/series), fix any rejects, and leave out a
     patch that upstream made unneeded (such as the scanner thread one past
     88c83b9). Then make the patches again against REF (commit each one in
     a scratch git repo of REF and git format-patch them), rewrite series,
     and change "d40912d" in their messages.
  4. Compare meson.build's dependency() lines and meson_options.txt with
     debian/control's Build-Depends and debian/rules' feature options.
  5. Update the commit above, and debian/copyright if protocols/ changed.
  6. dch -v VERSION -D trixie "New upstream ...", with VERSION
     1.0~betaN+flickos1 for a release, or 1.0~betaN+gitYYYYMMDD+flickos1
     for a commit after release N.
